WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.700
Good, morning, John.

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:08.760
Do you remember when you had that idea for making a channel, where you just review, like, not things but ideas of things?

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.800
Yeah, I was gonna call it "The Anthropocene Reviewed."

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.520
I'm gonna do that in this video. I'm just gonna do a bunch of 'em.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.580
-Great! I can't wait!
-See how it goes.
-I'm excited!

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.280
Let's start with the city of Springfield, Oregon as viewed from an airplane.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:27.040
Three stars, entirely without apparent intentionality. Too many rivers, making the space feel confined.

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:33.180
Also, the rivers' directionless meandering clashes with the right angles formed by the I-5 I-105 intersection.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:38.220
And finally, how can one be sure whether they're looking at Springfield or Eugene? Which is it?

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:43.020
I left it behind feeling confused and unsettled. I'm sure it's lovely close up, though.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.020
Orange traffic cones: five stars.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:52.820
Built simultaneously for durability, visibility, stability, stackability, and low cost, the orange traffic cone was almost an inevitability.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:59.380
Stability can be increased by adding heavy-duty rubber to the base and visibility can be increased by adding reflective collars.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:07.400
Orange traffic cones can survive vehicle impacts in excess of 65 miles per hour and still be usable, and yet, they cost less than a gin and tonic in Manhattan.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.240
These temporary traffic control devices have converged upon their best possible form.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.980
Half-drunk bottle of Fiji water: two stars.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:21.120
As we are not sure who drank the first half of this bottle of Fiji water, no one is enthusiastic about finishing it.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:26.420
Any one of us would no doubt take it down if there were no other options, but there are other options.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:31.909
The half-drunk state does, however, allow one to appreciate the care with which the design of the Fiji bottle

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:36.160
takes advantage of the refraction of the water to interact with the interior printing.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:43.040
But on the whole, I feel guilty that this water was shipped from Fiji and now, probably, no one is going to drink it.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.340
Socks: five stars.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:49.140
Socks are designed for absorbency, warmth, and to reduce friction.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.080
And, counter-intuitively, while the sock is one of humanity's most valuable creations,

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:57.280
the feeling of removing a sock at the end of the day is a five-star sensation as well.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:02.300
Socks' decorative properties, while not part of their core function, are high-quality as well.

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:05.460
They allow the wearer to make subtle, yet powerful statements.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:10.960
The first sock in the DFTBA Awesome Sox Club Subscription for 2017: five stars.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:18.480
Designed by Rachel Calderon Navarro, these socks are cushy, durable, and warm, the culmination of the testing of many different socks.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:24.240
Rachel's design sense makes these socks a subtle, yet surprising pop of color at the base of anyone's jeans.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:31.340
Sock club entrants are limited. You'll get a new pair of DFTBA socks every month, but you can only sign up during the month of November.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:34.580
This 1970s thermostat: 3.5 stars.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.960
These handcrafted thermostats really resonate with me.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:41.420
It's easy to understand how they were constructed by human hands

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:47.920
and the design elements were clearly built around the necessity of their form, which was limited by the injection molding techniques of the time.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:53.000
But oh, hmm, something seems to be off. There, that's better. Now we're up to four stars.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.940
One wonders how long that had been off kilter.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:00.360
Ah, and let me fix this as well. That's better. 4.5 stars.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.620
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

